{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/arjaythedev/multicore-server-challenge/pulls/32","id":4971512740,"node_id":"PR_kwDOVJRVo88AAAABKFNDpA","html_url":"https://github.com/arjaythedev/multicore-server-challenge/pull/32","diff_url":"https://github.com/arjaythedev/multicore-server-challenge/pull/32.diff","patch_url":"https://github.com/arjaythedev/multicore-server-challenge/pull/32.patch","issue_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/arjaythedev/multicore-server-challenge/issues/32","commits_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/arjaythedev/multicore-server-challenge/pulls/32/commits","review_comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/arjaythedev/multicore-server-challenge/pulls/32/comments","review_comment_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/arjaythedev/multicore-server-challenge/pulls/comments{/number}","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/arjaythedev/multicore-server-challenge/issues/32/comments","statuses_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/arjaythedev/multicore-server-challenge/statuses/23a14e8cbf32113509cd0ad941320ef947508981","number":32,"state":"open","locked":false,"title":"Add optimized Goose Conduit server with bounded SQL and commit batching","user":{"login":"tbsvttr","id":12001541,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjEyMDAxNTQx","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/12001541?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/tbsvttr","html_url":"https://github.com/tbsvttr","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/tbsvttr/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/tbsvttr/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/tbsvttr/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/tbsvttr/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/tbsvttr/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/tbsvttr/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/tbsvttr/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/tbsvttr/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/tbsvttr/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"The current optimized source is `23a14e8`: two event loops, eight PostgreSQL connections total, bounded read/commit collection, and statement-local SQL sharing. The previous published score is **#8 at 65,000 users**; Rust #7 leads at 100,000. That [official comparison](https://github.com/arjaythedev/multicore-server-challenge/blob/c3012257f2ee3dd2ccc72ada2eeba2b0d1278e8e/LEADERBOARD.md) predates this update. This revision needs a new official run; no first-place score is claimed.\n\nAdd a direct Goose implementation of the Conduit API, adapting our [single-core Goose PR #143](https://github.com/arjaythedev/twelve-dollar-server-challenge/pull/143) and [XeonFX’s MIT-licensed multicore PR #31](https://github.com/arjaythedev/multicore-server-challenge/pull/31), with batching attribution retained for [Scooter1337’s Rust PR #7](https://github.com/arjaythedev/multicore-server-challenge/pull/7). NOTICE.md pins the origins and LICENSE retains their terms. Only `submissions/goose-native-tbsvttr/` changes.\n\nThe optimization deduplicates compatible list/count work, shares metadata for actual selected feed articles, and batches feeds/comments without losing viewer flags, ordering or missing/empty behavior. Read pools wake after at most their configured collection window when eligible; writers collect for 1 ms before Sync and return success after commit. Bcrypt runs exactly the occupied lanes and ages collection from enqueue. Defaults are WORKERS=2, PG_READERS=3, HASHERS=2, ARTICLE_BATCH=1, READ_BATCH_US=5000 and WRITE_BATCH_US=1000.\n\nArticle updates and favorite/unfavorite responses reread committed metadata by immutable ID in a fresh statement snapshot, including concurrent slug renames. Legal embedded-NUL passwords retain all bytes and real cost-10 bcrypt work. Fresh JWT authentication runs on every authenticated request. There is no cross-request result cache, CPU affinity, schema/configuration change or worker restart; app and PostgreSQL share the challenge budget.\n\nValidation of the final ARM64 binary `7a7c31fb…` on Ubuntu 24.04 Docker/Mac:\n\n- Unchanged conformance: **197/197 on both dev and full seeds**, with original seed fingerprints.\n- **1,664** concurrent-read oracle requests across six read families, including actual batch-SQL witnesses, mixed viewers and committed changes.\n- Deferred-COMMIT/no-early-response, grouped rollback, quiet read/writer timers, concurrent favorite counts, update and favorite/rename races.\n- Embedded-NUL passwords interoperate both ways with pinned Go x/crypto/bcrypt at cost 10, including 72-byte UTF-8 input and wrong-prefix rejection.\n- **293,966 native assertions**, **4,000 Goose JWT assertions**, and **252 exact-lane bcrypt comparisons**.\n\nNative x86 validation **passed** in the [fork-only Ubuntu workflow](https://github.com/tbsvttr/multicore-server-challenge/actions/runs/39937895836): all 18 stages succeeded, including a clean pinned compiler/application build, four unchanged 197-step conformance runs, native/JWT/bcrypt checks, the read oracle, quiet/commit/rollback/race probes and trusted-Go NUL-password interoperability. Its submission Git tree `334be131` is identical to PR source `23a14e8`; validation commit `9457487` adds only fork validation files. Those files stay off this PR. The PR is ready for review. Upstream CI is waiting for maintainer approval before starting any jobs. The unchanged Rust control also passes all 197 full-seed checks on its exact local benchmark binary.\n\nThe [preserved measurement evidence](https://github.com/tbsvttr/multicore-server-challenge/tree/dd941e3285933eee82d7a9da4433ec18701032a9/benchmarks/2026-10-23/optimization) includes one matched **60-second, 100,000-user** point on the final binary. Both implementations passed native measurement guards with zero errors. Goose used **16.09% less combined app+PostgreSQL CPU per successful request** (0.160411 vs 0.191172 ms). Its p95/p99 were 6.603/46.751 ms versus Rust’s 15.703/78.463 ms, while its median, mean and maximum latency were higher. This is one exploratory point, not a stable improvement or capacity score.\n\nAll **five local capacity searches stopped before any 300-second hold**, due to generator scheduling failures or actual Mac sleep. The higher-load Goose collapse remains unresolved; invalid generation does not establish that the application is fault-free under that load. Raw errors, timing failures, native flags and every historical attempt remain in the archive. Follow-up diagnostics and a bounded-reader prototype are documented separately; that prototype is **not part of this PR** and has no performance result.\n\nApp and PostgreSQL shared **8 CPU / 16 GiB / no swap**, with original full seed, schema and durable database settings. The load generator had a separate container budget. This shared ARM64 Mac/Docker setup cannot establish the official dedicated x86 leaderboard rank. Further capacity testing was paused when the Mac began discharging at critical battery despite intermittent AC.\n\nThe [unified official comparison](https://github.com/tbsvttr/multicore-server-challenge/tree/benchmark-2026-10-22/benchmarks/2026-10-23) retains one row per current implementation, including unscored entries. Its existing official results remain unchanged. The [earlier measurement archive](https://github.com/tbsvttr/multicore-server-challenge/tree/benchmark-2026-10-22/benchmarks/2026-10-22) and the [new validation and measurement snapshot](https://github.com/tbsvttr/multicore-server-challenge/tree/dd941e3285933eee82d7a9da4433ec18701032a9/benchmarks/2026-10-23/optimization) preserve all raw attempts and their limitations. A new official run of source `23a14e8` is still required.\n","labels":[],"milestone":null,"active_lock_reason":null,"created_at":"2026-10-22T17:33:33Z","updated_at":"2026-10-23T18:05:13Z","closed_at":null,"merged_at":null,"merge_commit_sha":"04f1